<h1>Collating &amp; Laning Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A laning conveyor distributes a single-file product stream across multiple parallel lanes to feed case packers, tray loaders, and cartoners. A collating conveyor arranges products from those lanes into a specific pattern, a 3×4 array, a 2×5 group, or any count pattern the downstream machine requires. Shuttleworth, Kleenline, and Benchmark Automation build laning and collating systems for food, beverage, personal care, and pharmaceutical packaging lines running 50 to 600+ products per minute.</p>
<h2 id="diff-heading">Laning vs. Collating</h2>
<p>A laning conveyor turns one single-file stream into several parallel lanes, and a collating conveyor groups the product in those lanes into the count pattern a case packer or tray loader needs. The two functions often happen in sequence on the same conveyor section. Understanding the difference prevents specification errors:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Laning</strong> divides one stream into many. Product enters on a single lane at high speed and exits on two, three, four, or more lanes at proportionally lower speed. A 600 BPM single-file line laned into four lanes produces 150 BPM per lane. Laning happens upstream of the case packer.</li>
<li><strong>Collating</strong> arranges products within lanes into groups. A collating section gathers 12 bottles into a 3×4 pattern for case loading. Collating happens after laning and immediately before the case packer or <a href="/conveyor-functions/tray-loading/">tray loader</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>A typical bottle line runs: filler → capper → labeler → single-file conveyor → <strong>laning section</strong> → <strong>collating section</strong> → case packer. The laning section reduces line speed and distributes product. The collating section groups product into the pattern the case packer expects. Specifying these as one "laning conveyor" in an RFQ misses the collating requirement and often leads to unexpected project scope growth.</p>
<h2 id="methods-heading">Product Laning Methods</h2>
<p>Product laning machinery falls into four groups: chicane guide rails, servo lane-select diverters, speed-differential conveyors, and delta robots. Product shape, infeed rate, and changeover frequency decide which one fits. The <a href="#compare-heading">comparison table</a> summarizes the tradeoffs.</p>
<h3>Chicane (Serpentine) Laning</h3>
<p>Chicane laning routes product through S-shaped guide rails on a wide belt or tabletop conveyor. Product enters single-file on one side of the belt. Angled guide rails direct product into the first lane. When a downstream sensor detects that lane is full (or a counter triggers), a gate diverts subsequent product past the first lane and into the second. The process repeats for each lane.</p>
<p>Chicane systems excel with round containers, bottles, cans, jars, because the product rolls smoothly against guide rails without jamming. The conveyor belt runs wider than the combined lane width, and lane dividers create the individual lanes downstream. Chicane laning handles 200–600+ bottles per minute on high-speed beverage lines. Hartness (ITW) and Kleenline supply chicane laning systems for beverage and dairy applications.</p>
<p>The primary limitation of chicane laning is product shape. Square or rectangular packages do not slide smoothly against angled rails, they catch corners and jam. Non-round products require servo-controlled or pusher-based laning instead. Chicane systems also struggle with lightweight products that tip at the guide rail contact points. Adding back-pressure control or Shuttleworth-style slip-torque conveyors at the guide rails reduces tipping on lightweight containers.</p>
<h3>Servo Lane-Select Diverter</h3>
<p>Servo lane-select diverters use a servo-driven gate, pusher, or deflector to route each product to a specific lane. A sensor detects the incoming product, the PLC determines the target lane, and the servo actuator positions the diverter before the product arrives. This method handles round, square, rectangular, and irregular products because the diverter physically pushes or deflects each product rather than relying on guide rail contact.</p>
<p>Servo diverters run 50–200+ packages per minute depending on product spacing, diverter travel distance, and servo response time. They require more sophisticated controls than chicane systems, each diverter needs a servo drive, controller, and sensor integration with the line PLC. Benchmark Automation and Bastian Solutions build servo lane-select systems for case-packed goods, pouches, and cartons.</p>
<p>The advantage of servo lane selection is positive control. Every product goes to a known lane. Lane assignment can follow any algorithm, sequential (1-2-3-4-1-2-3-4), fill-and-switch, priority-based, or dynamic load balancing. This flexibility matters when lane fill rates differ (e.g., one case packer runs faster than the other) or when different SKUs route to different lanes for mixed-case packing.</p>
<h3>Speed-Differential Laning</h3>
<p>Speed-differential laning uses multiple belts running at different speeds to separate product into lanes. Product enters on a fast belt. A slower belt alongside creates a speed difference that pulls product sideways through an opening into the adjacent lane. This method is gentle, no mechanical pushers, gates, or guide rails contact the product.</p>
<p>Shuttleworth's slip-torque conveyors are the best-known implementation of speed-differential laning. Individual rollers slip under product that contacts a side rail or stop, allowing the conveyor to continue running without building back-pressure. Products naturally fill lanes in sequence as downstream lanes back up. This self-regulating behavior reduces controls complexity, no sensors, no PLC logic, no servo drives. The slip-torque principle makes Shuttleworth conveyors popular for fragile bakery, snack, and confectionery products that cannot tolerate contact pressure.</p>
<p>Speed-differential laning runs best at moderate speeds (50–200 PPM) and works for products with a flat, stable bottom surface. Products with high centers of gravity or narrow bases tend to tip during the lateral transfer. Adding low-profile side guides stabilizes tall products but adds changeover parts for different product widths.</p>
<h3>Robotic Laning (Delta Robot)</h3>
<p>Delta robots mounted above the conveyor pick products from a single-file infeed and place them directly into lanes, combining laning and collating in one step. Vision systems track product on the infeed belt, and the robot picks and places each product into the correct position within the lane/collation pattern. This approach eliminates separate laning and collating conveyor sections.</p>
<p>Robotic laning costs more than mechanical alternatives but excels in two scenarios: (1) very high SKU counts where mechanical changeover would consume too much time, and (2) random product orientation where the robot can orient product during the pick-place cycle. BluePrint Automation and ABB supply delta robot systems for combined laning/collating in bakery and snack applications.</p>
<h2 id="collating-heading">Collating Methods</h2>
<h3>Lane-Stop Collating</h3>
<p>The simplest collation method uses stops at the end of each lane. Product accumulates against the stop until the lane holds the correct count (e.g., 4 bottles per lane × 3 lanes = 12-count case pattern). When all lanes reach their count, the stops retract simultaneously and the collated group advances onto the case packer's infeed. This method requires <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation capability</a> in each lane to prevent back-pressure damage. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyor</a> zones or Shuttleworth slip-torque sections provide the zero-pressure accumulation needed for lane-stop collating.</p>
<h3>Sweep Collating</h3>
<p>Sweep collating uses a servo-driven pusher bar that sweeps a row of product from the lanes onto the case packer's bucket or dead plate. Product accumulates in lanes continuously. When the correct count fills each lane, the sweep bar pushes the entire row as a unit. Sweep collating is faster than lane-stop collating because product flow does not stop, the sweep bar moves with the lane conveyor during the push, maintaining continuous infeed.</p>
<p>Sweep collating handles 60–150+ cases per minute on high-speed lines. Kleenline and Benchmark Automation build sweep collating systems for beverage, dairy, and CPG applications. The critical design parameter is sweep bar timing, the bar must arrive at the push position precisely when all lanes have the correct product count. A mistimed sweep pushes an incomplete pattern, causing a case packer fault.</p>
<h3>Metering Collating</h3>
<p>Metering collating uses timed conveyor sections to group product. A slow metering belt feeds product into a faster spacing belt, creating gaps between groups. The gap size corresponds to the case packer's cycle time. This method avoids mechanical contact with product, no stops, no pushers, no sweep bars, making it ideal for fragile or delicate products. <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Indexing conveyors</a> with servo control provide the precise speed changes metering collation requires.</p>
