<h2 id="apps-heading">Industry Applications</h2>
<h3>Beverage Lines</h3>
<p>Beverage lines represent the highest-speed laning application. A typical bottling line runs 400–1,200 BPM (bottles per minute) through the filler and capper. The single-file output must lane into 4–8 lanes to feed case packers running at 30–60 cases per minute. Chicane laning with <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/beverage/">tabletop chain conveyors</a> handles the round containers at speed. Back-pressure management is critical, bottles backed up in chicane lanes can topple, jam, and halt the line. Hartness, Kleenline, and Intelligrated supply high-speed chicane laning for beverage.</p>
<h3>Bakery &amp; Snack Foods</h3>
<p>Bakery lines lane cookies, crackers, bars, and pastries from oven discharge into multi-lane <a href="/conveyor-functions/tray-loading/">tray loading</a> stations. Products are fragile, irregularly shaped, and arrive from the oven in random orientation. Shuttleworth slip-torque conveyors provide gentle laning without contact pressure. Delta robot cells combine laning and tray loading into a single step. Benchmark Automation integrates bakery laning with downstream case packing.</p>
<h3>Case Packing</h3>
<p>Every drop-packer, wrap-around case packer, and robotic case packer requires product collated into the correct count and pattern. The laning/collating section is the bridge between the single-file packaging line and the case packer. A 24-count case with a 4×6 pattern needs 4-lane collating. A 12-count case with a 3×4 pattern needs 3-lane collating. Changing case counts requires laning and collating changeover, the fewer mechanical adjustments required, the faster the changeover. Servo-controlled laning and electronic collation recipe changes support rapid format changes in multi-SKU operations.</p>
<h3>Pharmaceutical &amp; Personal Care</h3>
<p>Pharmaceutical cartons, tubes, and bottles lane into collating sections that feed case packers. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Sanitary requirements</a> may apply for topical products and oral care. Servo lane-select diverters handle the rectangular product shapes (cartons, tubes) that chicane systems cannot. Traceability requirements in pharmaceutical applications mean each product's lane assignment may need to be logged for batch records, servo diverters with PLC logging satisfy this requirement.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Selecting a Laning &amp; Collating System</h2>
<p>Start with five questions that narrow the technology and configuration:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>What is the infeed rate?</strong> Products per minute entering the laning section. Divide by the number of output lanes to calculate per-lane rate. Ensure the case packer can handle the per-lane rate.</li>
<li><strong>What product shape?</strong> Round containers suit chicane laning. Square/rectangular products need servo diverters or speed-differential systems. Fragile products need Shuttleworth slip-torque or delta robots.</li>
<li><strong>How many output lanes?</strong> Determined by the case pattern. A 24-count case in a 4×6 pattern needs 4 lanes. Changing case counts may require changing lane count, design for the maximum lane count and blank off unused lanes for smaller patterns.</li>
<li><strong>How many SKUs and how often do you change over?</strong> Chicane systems with fixed guide rails require 15–30 minutes for product-width changeover. Servo diverters change over in 1–2 minutes via recipe. Delta robots change over in seconds.</li>
<li><strong>What case packer type feeds downstream?</strong> Drop packers need products collated in a tightly grouped pattern from above. Wrap-around packers need products collated on a flat surface. Robotic case packers may accept product in lanes without collating. Match the laning/collating output to the case packer's infeed requirement.</li>
</ol>
<p>Budget the laning and collating section as 15–25% of the total packaging line cost. Lines that underinvest in this section suffer chronic jams, miscounts, and case packer faults. For cost planning, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>. For broader automation context, see the <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems</a> guide.</p>
